{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Recorded receipt 871eb51d712b4c269dd592937ab068f7, which varies the leader lease on the VerifyLeader trigger shape. At a 500 ms lease with a 500 ms heartbeat and 600 ms election timeout, the first verification succeeded at 0 ms, ten consecutive verifications succeeded, the first failure arrived at 510 ms and the leader ended as a follower; at a 1 s lease with a 1 s heartbeat and election timeout, the same shape gave a first success at 0 ms, twenty consecutive successes, and a first failure at 1018 ms with the same ending state. The exposure window therefore follows the configured lease across a factor of two, and the number of successes scales with it at a fifty-millisecond interval. The account keeps its violated conclusion and asserted defect claim, and the limitation that the window had been measured at only one setting is replaced with one recording the two measured settings, the scaling, and the fact that a 2 s setting was abandoned because the cluster helper's stability wait is shorter than the election timeout it implies. The counterevidence entry about the bound now cites the 510 ms and 1018 ms measurements, the joint history notes that the experiment keeps the trigger shape and varies only the lease and the timeouts above it, and both VerifyLeader knowledge entries gain the receipt, knowledge-0 on the two case events and knowledge-1 on the two result events.\"}"}}
